    "case_id": "431",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s presented with new and progressive satellite lesions while undergoing pembrolizumab therapy. Two years earlier, he had undergone a wide local excision with graft repair for a left posterior auricular scalp lesion. An incidentally resected lymph node was found to be focally involved, with no evidence of distant metastatic disease. Within months, multiple unresectable satellite lesions appeared. He received 4 cycles of ipilimumab, followed by a second wide local excision when new lesions appeared. Additional satellite lesions developed and he began pembrolizumab therapy, but after 5 cycles, local progression occurred in the satellite lesions.\n\nMultiple satellite lesions were present on the left posterior auricular scalp area. After treatment, clinically regressed lesions with persistent black pigmentation were noted.\n\n- Histopathology:    Initial findings: Incidentally resected lymph node showed focal involvement        Post-treatment biopsy findings:    - H&E sections showed intense mixed superficial and deep dermal inflammatory infiltrate composed of numerous neutrophils, histiocytes, eosinophils, and lymphocytes    - Immunostaining results:      * SOX-10 and MART-1: Highlighted normal melanocytes in basal layer of epidermis only      * CD163 and myeloperoxidase: Highlighted histiocytes and neutrophils respectively        Images referenced:    Figure 1. Title: Recurrent Melanoma Lesions on the Left Posterior Auricular Scalp Before and After T-VEC Therapy    Figure 2. Title: Histopathologic Findings of T-VEC–Treated Melanoma Lesions    Description: Shows complete tumor regression and neutrophil-rich mixed inflammation; MART-1 immunostain confirms complete tumor regression",
